WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:09.780
On a July day in the Chesapeake Bay, a buoy at Stingray Point, Virginia, measured

2
00:00:09.780 --> 00:00:11.200
the air and the water.

3
00:00:12.040 --> 00:00:14.920
The air warmed by about 8 degrees Celsius.

4
00:00:15.860 --> 00:00:20.080
The water just under the buoy changed by less than one and a half.

5
00:00:21.220 --> 00:00:26.380
Why does water behave so strangely, and how does that help keep living things alive?

6
00:00:27.333 --> 00:00:33.973
A water molecule has one oxygen atom and two hydrogen atoms, joined by covalent

7
00:00:33.973 --> 00:00:39.013
bonds. A covalent bond is a bond where atoms share electrons.

8
00:00:40.253 --> 00:00:42.773
Oxygen pulls the shared electrons closer.

9
00:00:43.313 --> 00:00:48.613
So the oxygen end is slightly negative, and the hydrogen ends are slightly positive.

10
00:00:49.473 --> 00:00:51.893
A molecule like that is called polar.

11
00:00:52.883 --> 00:00:54.663
Opposite charges attract.

12
00:00:55.483 --> 00:00:59.403
The hydrogen of one molecule is drawn to the oxygen of a neighbor.

13
00:01:00.483 --> 00:01:04.203
This weak attraction between molecules is a hydrogen bond.

14
00:01:05.163 --> 00:01:09.203
In liquid water, hydrogen bonds form and break all the time.

15
00:01:10.003 --> 00:01:13.243
They are much weaker than the covalent bonds inside a molecule.

16
00:01:14.033 --> 00:01:17.593
A solvent is a liquid that dissolves other substances.

17
00:01:18.613 --> 00:01:21.193
Water's charged ends pull salt apart.

18
00:01:22.513 --> 00:01:25.593
Cohesion is the attraction of water molecules to each other.

19
00:01:26.353 --> 00:01:30.613
At the surface it gives surface tension, which can hold up a water strider.

20
00:01:31.753 --> 00:01:35.633
Adhesion is the attraction of water to a different substance, such as paper.

21
00:01:36.567 --> 00:01:42.807
Specific heat is the energy needed to warm one gram of a substance by one degree

22
00:01:42.807 --> 00:01:48.067
Celsius. For liquid water it is about 4.18 joules.

23
00:01:48.527 --> 00:01:51.307
For dry sand it is about 0.8.

24
00:01:52.307 --> 00:01:57.227
Part of the energy added to water goes into stretching and breaking hydrogen bonds.

25
00:01:57.487 --> 00:02:00.407
So less of it shows up as a rise in temperature.

26
00:02:01.527 --> 00:02:07.967
100 grams of water that takes in 836.8 joules warms by 2 degrees.

27
00:02:08.927 --> 00:02:12.107
The same energy would warm dry sand by about 10.

28
00:02:13.050 --> 00:02:16.990
Heat capacity is the same idea for a whole object.

29
00:02:17.610 --> 00:02:22.330
A big body of water, like the Bay, has an enormous heat capacity.

30
00:02:22.630 --> 00:02:24.790
So it changes temperature slowly.

31
00:02:25.630 --> 00:02:31.670
The fish, crabs, and grasses in it usually face gentler daily swings than the air

32
00:02:31.670 --> 00:02:34.770
over land does. Water is polar.

33
00:02:35.570 --> 00:02:38.230
Hydrogen bonds hold its molecules near each other.

34
00:02:38.770 --> 00:02:41.550
That helps explain why water warms slowly.
